    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This thesis examines the strategies employed by the liberal-minded Rural Development Group to influence China’s central government policy between 1978 and 1984. Facing ideological restrictions, the group adopted the language of cybernetics to advocate for liberal economic policies and simultaneously institutionalize their roles as independent researchers within the Communist Party’s rigid bureaucracy. With the endorsement of cybernetics as a Marxist science by China’s top military scientists, the group’s researchers articulated the challenges of Chinese postsocialist governance as technical exercises in feedback and command. Leveraging the perceived scientific neutrality of the cybernetic sciences, they amassed political influence in Premier Zhao Ziyang’s government. This history illustrates the spontaneous rise of technocratic institutions in the early Chinese reform in contestation with other forms of political participation. It also highlights the ideological fluidity of cybernetics as a tool for the intellectual elite to navigate postsocialist transitions.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Fang, T. M. (2024). Cybernetic Reformers: Young Intellectuals and Rural Research in Post-Mao China. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/gw946sh9743. https://doi.org/10.25740/gw946sh9743.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
